  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 319 AM MST WED NOV 25 2026\nHigh pressure will build into the region on Sunday, bringing a \nbrief respite from the wet holiday week. This looks to be short-\nlived, as another shortwave trough moves into the region on Monday \nand Tuesday. This will bring slightly cooler temperatures, lower \nsnow levels back down to 5000-6000 ft MSL, and another push of \nvalley rain and mountain snow. Ridging will attempt to build back in \non Wednesday, but a closed low just off the coast of California will \nmove through the region, splitting the upper level flow and bringing \ncooler air and precipitation to the southern tier of the region. \nModel uncertainty remains on the timing and positioning of the low, \nbut most solutions and ensemble members agree that the bulk of the \nprecipitation will be to our south over the Desert Southwest. \nCurrent forecast keeps a 20-40% chance of precipitation over the \nregion through Wednesday, with temperatures lingering slightly above \nnormal. Interestingly enough, the potential for a hard freeze in the \nBoise metro area appears to still not occur by early December. This \nwould be the latest freeze on record, with the previous latest \nfreeze occurring November 16th, 2016.",
